{0 How it works}

A workero Worker is a WebAssembly module that a small JavaScript entry point
loads and calls. You write only OCaml.

{1 Compilation: wasm_of_ocaml}

[(modes wasm)] in your [dune] file makes dune compile the OCaml bytecode with
{{:https://ocsigen.org/js_of_ocaml/}wasm_of_ocaml}. It gives a loader
([<name>.bc.wasm.js]) and a directory of [.wasm] files
([<name>.bc.wasm.assets]). OCaml 5 effects work in this backend.

{1 JavaScript plumbing}

A Worker has to import its [.wasm] files, because the runtime gives a
precompiled [WebAssembly.Module] and does not allow compiling wasm from bytes.
[workero build] writes two files for this into [_worker/]:

- [load-wasm.js] gives the wasm_of_ocaml loader the precompiled modules and
  waits for OCaml initialisation.
- [index.js] imports every [.wasm] file, calls the loader, and exports the
  handlers that your program registered. See {{!page-handlers}Handlers}.

The OCaml library reaches the runtime APIs ([Request], [Response], [fetch],
bindings) through [Js_of_ocaml]. Workero converts between OCaml values and
JavaScript objects. For APIs without a wrapper, see
{{!page-interop}JavaScript interop}.

{1 Waiting for JavaScript}

A Worker isolate has one JavaScript thread and event loop. Its WebAssembly
module loads once per isolate, and many requests can use it. JavaScript I/O
returns Promises; WebAssembly cannot block the thread while one settles.

Workero runs each handler in an OCaml 5 effect handler. When
{!Workero.Promise.await} performs an effect, that handler captures the rest of
the OCaml call as a continuation. It attaches callbacks to the Promise and
returns control to the JavaScript event loop, so other requests can progress.
On fulfillment, a callback resumes the continuation with the value. On
rejection, it resumes by raising the error. The handler can suspend again.
When the handler returns a {!Workero.Response.t} or raises, its outer Promise
settles and the runtime handles the response or error.

wasm_of_ocaml implements these effects with WebAssembly's JavaScript Promise
Integration (JSPI). The suspended continuation includes a WebAssembly stack.
WebAssembly can suspend only when JavaScript enters it through a
[WebAssembly.promising] function. The entry into each handler and every resume
use this boundary. Workero handles this mechanism; handler code uses direct
style. See {{!page-async}Async and background work} for practical use and limits.

{1 Bundling: workero build}

[workero build src/main.bc.wasm.js] builds the executable (in the release profile unless you
pass another profile), checks that it has no missing primitives, and writes the
directory that Wrangler bundles and deploys. See {{!page-cli}the CLI}. The
template runs it as the [[build]] command of [wrangler.toml].

{1 Binary optimization: wasm-opt}

wasm_of_ocaml runs [wasm-opt] from binaryen on the output, which is why binaryen
must be on [PATH]. Adding libraries can increase the module size. See
{{!page-limits}Limits} for current platform limits.
